---
Universal, zero-dependency linter & formatter

Usage: poly [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  lint        Lint files (poly applies no fixes without --fix)
  fmt         Format files (dry-run by default; use --fix to write in place)
  commit      Lint and optionally clean a commit message (reads `[commit]` from poly.toml)
  hooks       Run git hooks declared in `[hooks]` of poly.toml (native runner)
  migrate     Absorb foreign tool configs into poly.toml and remove what poly can honor
  cache       Inspect and maintain the result cache (stats / gc / clean)
  rules       Inspect and test custom ast-grep rule packs (test / list)
  components  Inspect and manage downloadable engine components (list / status / install / sync /
              prune)
  lock        Pin remote `extends` bases and Git hook sources in `poly.lock`
  config      Inspect the effective config and print the `poly.toml` JSON Schema
  mcp         Run an MCP server over stdio (mirrors the CLI)
  doctor      Report which poly is running, every poly on PATH, and the config in effect
  help        Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --config <PATH>
          Path to a config file (default: nearest poly.toml)

      --color <WHEN>
          When to colour output. `auto` honours `NO_COLOR` and TTY detection

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour only when stdout is a terminal and `NO_COLOR` is unset
          - always: Always colour, even when piped
          - never:  Never colour
          
          [default: auto]

      --offline
          Never use the network: no component, grammar, catalog-tool, remote `extends` or
          hook-source fetches. Also set by `POLY_OFFLINE=1`

  -q, --quiet
          Trim `pretty` output to the findings and the summary: drop the per-file discovery and skip
          detail printed beneath it.
          
          Every count and reason stays — the summary still reports how many files were linted, how
          many were skipped and why, and what discovery excluded. Only the itemised list of paths
          goes away. Findings still print and exit codes are unchanged. No effect on the `--format
          json`/`toon` document. Mutually exclusive with `--verbose` and `--debug`.

  -v, --verbose
          Show extra per-finding detail in `pretty` output: description, rule URL, and metadata, and
          list every skipped file instead of the first few. For `--format json`/`toon` the findings
          are always fully structured, so this only lifts the cap on the skip note printed to stderr

      --debug
          Emit debug data: per-engine cache hit/miss and timing (shown in `pretty`, attached to
          `json`/`toon`), and raise log verbosity to `debug` on stderr.
          
          The top of the verbosity ladder, so it implies `--verbose`.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
